(350758, 26729, 259, '<td valign="top">man you really did have to crop in quite a bit.  i had to do the same sorta thing with my picture also.  however, not nearly as much.  nice job, i had you scored with someone else (not the winner, and not even close either) for my highest this week.</td>', 'man you really did have to crop in quite a bit.  i had to do the same sorta thing with my picture also.  however, not nearly as much.  nice job, i had you scored with someone else (not the winner, and not even close either) for my highest this week.', '2003-07-09 02:59:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(350797, 27138, 4411, '<td valign="top">Guess which ''golden rule'' I broke with this one : )<br/>Thanks for the comments, but here''s my take on it:<br/> <br/>This is actually quite a generous crop as far as motorsport photography goes - you should see the shots I didn''t dare enter!<br/> <br/>Motorsort fans generally want to see the car and driver in as much detail as possible and very little else (unless there''s a pit babe hanging around).<br/> <br/>The idea of a panning shot is to give the impression that the frame is moving with the car - so there''s no need to leave space in the frame for the car to move into.</td>', 'Guess which ''golden rule'' I broke with this one : )Thanks for the comments, but here''s my take on it: This is actually quite a generous crop as far as motorsport photography goes - you should see the shots I didn''t dare enter! Motorsort fans generally want to see the car and driver in as much detail as possible and very little else (unless there''s a pit babe hanging around). The idea of a panning shot is to give the impression that the frame is moving with the car - so there''s no need to leave space in the frame for the car to move into.', '2003-07-09 03:35:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(350799, 26809, 9109, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the comments. This is my first submission and am very happy about my results, although I will strive to do better in future challenges.<br/> <br/>For those who asked how I did it, it was just a matter of using long exposure (4 seconds) and pushing the model car forward with my hand. I stopped the car for the last 1 second or so to obtain the final sharp image.<br/> <br/>Lighting was a problem because I didn''t have any proper studio setup for this shot. This was done entirely on a table lined with heavy black cardboard for the background. There was one fluorescent tube light directly overhead, one on the wall to the left and one more at a staircase behind. Also the afternoon sun was shining through a glass window directly above the table (about maybe 16-18 feet high?). After experimenting with some settings, I just ended up turning off all the lights except the staircase one which got reflected nicely on the rear view mirror.<br/> <br/>I actually experimented with a few other settings including a "double vision" version where there are two in-focus images and a blur in between, and one where the car sped from left to right but thought it was too cliched. In the end I decided on this one.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-07-09 02:40:53.</i></td>', 'Thanks for all the comments. This is my first submission and am very happy about my results, although I will strive to do better in future challenges. For those who asked how I did it, it was just a matter of using long exposure (4 seconds) and pushing the model car forward with my hand. I stopped the car for the last 1 second or so to obtain the final sharp image. Lighting was a problem because I didn''t have any proper studio setup for this shot. This was done entirely on a table lined with heavy black cardboard for the background. There was one fluorescent tube light directly overhead, one on the wall to the left and one more at a staircase behind. Also the afternoon sun was shining through a glass window directly above the table (about maybe 16-18 feet high?). After experimenting with some settings, I just ended up turning off all the lights except the staircase one which got reflected nicely on the rear view mirror. I actually experimented with a few other settings including a "double vision" version where there are two in-focus images and a blur in between, and one where the car sped from left to right but thought it was too cliched. In the end I decided on this one.', '2003-07-09 03:36:48.000000', '2003-07-09 03:40:53.000000', 0),
