(381347, 26906, 259, '<td valign="top">in a response to bosie (if anyone ever looks again).  i was panning at an extremely slow shutter speed in part because i wanted to get a panning image so the background was blured.  on a bright afternoon i wouldn''t have shot so slow, however, it was at dusk and there wasn''t enough light to shoot at a faster shutter speed and still have a normaly exposed shot.  i was reading in a national geographic photography help book made up of their photographers a couple of weeks after i shot this, and they said to start around 1/250th of a sec for racing.  and the better you get the slower you can go.  however, i had to make a huge compromise because of the lack of light.  so i had to be all that much steadier.  personaly i am pretty suprised about it now when i think about it.  it should have been much harder than it seemed at the time.</td>', 'in a response to bosie (if anyone ever looks again).  i was panning at an extremely slow shutter speed in part because i wanted to get a panning image so the background was blured.  on a bright afternoon i wouldn''t have shot so slow, however, it was at dusk and there wasn''t enough light to shoot at a faster shutter speed and still have a normaly exposed shot.  i was reading in a national geographic photography help book made up of their photographers a couple of weeks after i shot this, and they said to start around 1/250th of a sec for racing.  and the better you get the slower you can go.  however, i had to make a huge compromise because of the lack of light.  so i had to be all that much steadier.  personaly i am pretty suprised about it now when i think about it.  it should have been much harder than it seemed at the time.', '2003-08-07 22:28:16.000000', NULL, 0),
